WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:00.049 --> 00:00:02.500
Hi, guys! Dan for BBC Learning English here.

00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:04.460
Today, we're going to talk about the verbs

00:00:04.460 --> 00:00:06.240
'wish' and 'hope'.

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:09.400
Now, the verb 'wish' can be used in many ways

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:11.740
– and you can get much more detailed information

00:00:11.740 --> 00:00:12.780
on our website.

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:15.300
However, we generally use 'wish' to talk about

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:18.900
a present or past situation that we want to change,

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:21.600
but understand that it can't.

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:24.429
Present wishes are 'wish' + a past simple verb.

00:00:24.429 --> 00:00:24.980
For example:

00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:26.940
I wish I were taller.

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:30.140
Past wishes are 'wish' + past perfect.

00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:32.020
I wish I had grown taller.

00:00:32.440 --> 00:00:35.560
'Hope' can also be used in different ways. But generally,

00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:37.480
when we hope, we talk about something

00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:40.100
that we want or expect for the future.

00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:42.220
'Hope' can be followed by a full infinitive.

00:00:42.220 --> 00:00:43.020
For example:

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:44.700
I hope to see you soon.

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:46.560
But more commonly, we follow it with a

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:50.660
present tense that has a future meaning, or with 'will'.

00:00:50.660 --> 00:00:52.820
I hope you pass your driving test.

00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:55.180
I hope you'll have a good time.

